> Write a single-file, production-ready Python 3.12 CLI tool using **libcst** that strips comments, docstrings, and (optionally) type annotations from Python files in place. Fully typed, no docstringe, no comments, `loguru` for logging, `pathlib` for all paths, `multiprocessing.Pool` (default 4 workers, --pool-method=imap_unordered
> **Flags:** no flags → remove only inline/trailing comments; `-c/--comments` → all comments; `-d/--docstrings` → function/class docstrings; `-a/--all` → shebang + module docstring + all comments + all docstrings + type annotations; `-n/--stats` → per-file removal counts; `--dry-run`; `--diff`; `--backup` / `--backup-dir`; `--workers N`; `-q`/`-v`.
> **Preserve unless `-a`:** shebang, module docstring, `# type:` / `# fmt:` / encoding-cookie comments, and commented-out code blocks that parse as valid Python after `#` removal.
> **Correctness:** docstring as sole body statement → replace with `pass`. Validate every result with `ast.parse(..., feature_version=(3,12))` before writing. Writes atomic (temp file + `os.replace`), preserving mode. Read as bytes: `utf-8`, fall back to `cp1252`, else skip. Never crash on a single file — return a `FileResult` dataclass and continue.
> **Report:** bytes saved per file + total (default); comment/docstring counts (`-n`); end-of-run summary of scanned/modified/skipped/failed; exit codes 0/1/2. Sort output by path for determinism. Include `--include`/`--exclude` globs, `--check` (CI mode, no writes), `--json`, `--version`, `--no-recursive`. Handle extension-less files, CRLF/BOM/empty files, read-only files, and be idempotent.
